  "educational": {
    "scientificImportance": "Rice feeds more than half the world's population and is the second sequenced crop genome; its paddy-flood ecology creates unique anaerobic soil chemistry that concentrates arsenic and cadmium via the same silicon and phosphate transporters used for nutrient uptake.",
    "studentRelevance": "Rice makes the food-chain metal contamination story concrete at global scale — the same biology that makes it a wetland survivor also makes it the most important single dietary source of inorganic arsenic exposure for 3 billion people.",
    "indoorOutdoor": "outdoor",
    "realWorldIssue": "Flooded paddy soils mobilize arsenite and cadmium into pore water, where OsNramp5 and OsABCC1 transporters concentrate them in grain — making rice irrigation water quality a direct public-health variable."
  },
